- Unsloth excels at memory-efficient SFT/LoRA and some preference/GRPO variants in TRL-style loops, but does not natively provide multi-turn agentic RL with external tool environments at the scale of VERL. For RTX 5090 32GB, full agentic RL rollout with tool server will need aggressive downsizing (smaller group sizes, shorter lengths, fewer turns, 4-bit QLoRA, LoRA adapters, low micro-batches).
- A pragmatic approach is: (1) Perform the non-reasoning SFT using Unsloth with full VRAM optimizations; (2) If RL is desired, implement a pared-down GRPO-RoC loop leveraging Unsloth/TRL components for policy + reference models, and integrate tool calls via a lightweight local code runner or the provided Code Judge but at reduced concurrency. Expose checkpoints/eval/caching features as requested.

Q&A Log:
- 2025-09-14T11:06Z user: "model_name_or_path - can this also take a local path?"
  answer: Yes. Set `model.model_name_or_path` to an absolute or relative local directory containing HF Transformers-format weights and tokenizer (e.g., `/home/nir/models/gemma-3-12b-it`). Both AutoTokenizer and Unsloth FastLanguageModel accept local directories. For dockerized vLLM, ensure the same path is volume-mounted in docker-compose (`MODEL_PATH`) so the container can read it.
- 2025-09-14T11:20Z user: "how is the judge going to be running exactly? locally? can i configure it to use any base_url / api_key / model_name?"
  answer: By default, Code Judge runs locally via Docker Compose (`docker-compose.rstar.yml`) at `http://localhost:8088`, and the helper uses `localhost:8088` (see `rstar2_agent/tools/code_judge_utils.py:run_tool_calls_on_server_async`). You can point it to a remote judge by passing `host_addr`/`host_port` when calling that function or by adding env/config plumbing. For the LLM, the example client `examples/chat_with_tool_call.py` posts to `http://localhost:8000/v1/completions` and sets `payload["model"]` to the model; to use remote/self-hosted providers, change the `url` to your `base_url`, add an `Authorization: Bearer <api_key>` header if required, and set `payload["model"]` to the remote model name. Many providers expose an OpenAI-compatible API; ensure the endpoint matches the used API (completions vs chat/completions).
- 2025-09-14T11:45Z user: "where do i configure openrouter url/api key/model for judge? and MATH-500 keys?"
  answer: Code Judge does not call the LLM and therefore does not need OpenRouter config. It only executes model-generated code at `http://<judge_host>:<judge_port>`. Configure OpenRouter in the LLM client (e.g., `examples/chat_with_tool_call.py`) via flags `--base_url https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions --api_key $OPENROUTER_API_KEY --remote_model "nvidia/nemotron-nano-9b-v2:free"`, optionally `--api_kind chat` if supported. For MATH-500 JSONL, set `data.input_key: problem` and `data.target_key: answer` in `configs/sft_unsloth.yaml`.
- 2025-09-14T12:10Z user: "can learning_rate be set as decimal value and not scientific notation?"
  answer: Yes. YAML and Transformers accept either scientific (e.g., `2.0e-4`) or fixed-decimal (e.g., `0.0002`) forms. Both parse as float and work the same in `TrainingArguments`. If you prefer readability, set `training.learning_rate: 0.0002`.
